01:20:08.260 --> 01:20:15.240
But this one right now, where I am, I think that I've come full circle, and I do also want to vote this Essential.

01:20:15.700 --> 01:20:18.660
Okay, Sean, you can't stop it, but I don't think you were going to anyway.

01:20:18.760 --> 01:20:20.700
What's your vote? Yeah.

01:20:21.680 --> 01:20:32.920
It's funny how, I guess I kind of tipped my hand, and Mike kind of said exactly what I said at the beginning of the episode, to put this on the Essential Games list.

01:20:35.100 --> 01:20:36.440
But it's funny.

01:20:36.540 --> 01:20:39.000
We didn't talk about this during the episode.

01:20:39.540 --> 01:20:44.400
We usually talk about, oh, what was the game like on the Famicom?

01:20:44.400 --> 01:20:47.920
And it seemed like a lot more friendly of a game.

01:20:48.600 --> 01:20:54.640
Like, you know, Grant had, like, a totally overpowered attack.

01:20:55.000 --> 01:21:00.240
And there were some changes that just made the game easier.

01:21:00.240 --> 01:21:09.480
And in some ways, I kind of wish we got that game instead of the version that we were dealt because of Blockbuster or whatever you want to say.

01:21:10.800 --> 01:21:22.680
But I still think that the game that we got, as difficult as it is, and as frustrating as it may be, was just as frustrating as in the first one.

01:21:22.820 --> 01:21:26.640
And we were all frustrated, but we all really liked being frustrated.

01:21:26.640 --> 01:21:32.120
It's like, you know, I've said it in many episodes, it's very much the Dark Souls effect.

01:21:32.300 --> 01:21:35.640
Like, this hurts so good, in a way.

01:21:37.100 --> 01:21:42.760
But yeah, I'm also going to vote this on the Essential Games list for the same reasons that were stated.

01:21:43.660 --> 01:21:47.060
This is the first game, but more.

01:21:47.900 --> 01:21:59.040
The fact they leaned into, like, those set pieces in their level design, like Joe was saying, was really cool.

01:21:59.360 --> 01:22:05.500
Like, the way that they were kind of defying expectations on what a level, a Castlevania level could be.

01:22:06.720 --> 01:22:13.200
I really appreciated, and I thought it was an expansion on what we had previously experienced.

01:22:13.840 --> 01:22:21.940
I will not forgive its elementary school level opening crawl, but I think that's endearing.

01:22:23.180 --> 01:22:26.600
But yeah, I'm putting this on the Essential Games list as well.

01:22:27.780 --> 01:22:28.840
I'm helping.

01:22:30.360 --> 01:22:31.760
You're doing your part.

01:22:31.940 --> 01:22:33.020
I'm doing my part.

01:22:33.080 --> 01:22:34.560
I voted. Yes.

01:22:35.500 --> 01:22:40.040
I will say, Sean got me, you said a couple things there that just got me thinking,

01:22:40.200 --> 01:22:44.980
oh, I could do another 30 minutes on this show now, because you've given me a bunch of new topics to talk about.

01:22:45.520 --> 01:22:51.780
I'll start with, first off, just weird take here, but, like, I'll still take the first one over this right now.

01:22:51.900 --> 01:22:55.080
Like, if I'm being honest with myself, like, I still think the first game.

01:22:55.240 --> 01:22:59.260
And maybe that's because I played it on Nostalgia, and now I have Nostalgia.

01:22:59.260 --> 01:23:07.320
But, like, right now, one over three for me, in terms of, like, what I would pick up if I had to, you know, like, I'm by myself.

01:23:07.320 --> 01:23:08.800
I think it's just more classic now.

01:23:09.000 --> 01:23:17.000
Like, those levels, those moments are like, oh, yeah, that clock tower level, not this clock tower level.

01:23:17.440 --> 01:23:18.920
Yeah. So they're both essential.

01:23:19.240 --> 01:23:25.480
I'm just saying, like, for me, it's like, I'm just putting a, for anybody who was interested, for me, it goes one, three, two right now.

01:23:25.560 --> 01:23:29.820
And if I had to include four in there, I won't do that.

01:23:29.820 --> 01:23:32.420
Because, yeah, I won't do that, because we might get there someday.

01:23:32.980 --> 01:23:35.500
Ugh. Yeah.

01:23:35.700 --> 01:23:37.240
Pat and pending 2070.

01:23:37.660 --> 01:23:38.640
We'll get to that.

01:23:40.400 --> 01:23:44.660
And then the other thing I was going to say is you mentioned the Famicom version of the game.

01:23:44.980 --> 01:23:54.540
And, yes, that has some actual gameplay differences, but it also has a special, like, audio chip.

01:23:54.540 --> 01:23:59.840
Yeah. So it can play, it has two extra waveforms on it or something like that.

01:23:59.980 --> 01:24:01.180
I don't know how those, wave channels.

01:24:03.120 --> 01:24:10.200
And I listened to those tracks after I played through the NES version, and they're not good.

01:24:11.120 --> 01:24:15.160
Wow. I don't, it's the same, but it happened with Simon's Quest as well.

01:24:15.320 --> 01:24:19.100
Like, I feel like it's like, maybe if I heard them first, I'd be like, yeah, those are my songs.

01:24:19.100 --> 01:24:29.940
But, like, now, okay, so there are, there are, like, three amazing tracks in Castlevania 3, which is funny, because there's, like, seven in Castlevania 1.

01:24:30.260 --> 01:24:31.320
And there's two.

01:24:31.320 --> 01:24:32.380
That's neither here nor there.

01:24:32.600 --> 01:24:37.340
Yeah, there's two of the greatest of all time tracks in Castlevania 2.

01:24:37.900 --> 01:24:46.820
But in Castlevania 3, the track called The Beginning, which is the music for the first stage, that is, that is just like, wow, they did it again.

01:24:46.820 --> 01:24:53.120
For the third game in a row, we have just an absolute classic track that's just gonna stick with you.

01:24:53.400 --> 01:24:58.700
And it just doesn't sound like that in the Famicom version, like, because of these two extra channels.

01:24:58.800 --> 01:25:00.480
Yeah, I'm not expressing any opinion on those.

01:25:00.660 --> 01:25:05.960
I have, because, again, I only heard them very briefly, so I don't have that attachment.

01:25:05.960 --> 01:25:13.360
But it seems that the prevailing opinion outside of Mike is that they are better.

01:25:13.780 --> 01:25:17.340
I'm not saying I agree with that, but that is outside of the norm.

01:25:18.000 --> 01:25:25.880
Yeah, because it's just, like, really interesting that they put an additional chip onto there to, like, they didn't do that here for anything, as far as I know.

01:25:26.000 --> 01:25:32.600
Like, we didn't, the Famicom disk system was the reason for the second game having the extra music.

01:25:32.780 --> 01:25:36.080
But they didn't add this coprosser chip here, so that's interesting.

01:25:36.080 --> 01:25:48.320
And then the third thing I was gonna say that Sean had me thinking about was that there are, like, there's still some funny charm things, funny charming things about this game as well, like in the first one.

01:25:48.400 --> 01:25:55.860
If you guys remember in the first one, there was, like, you could pose in certain places to, like, have bags of money appear.

01:25:56.420 --> 01:26:00.820
Yeah. Or, like, hit the walls and there would be, like, a fully cooked turkey in there.

01:26:01.020 --> 01:26:03.660
Yeah, uh-huh. You know, like, that stuff is still present in this game, too.

01:26:03.860 --> 01:26:12.620
But also, you can enter as your name on the screen, HELP ME, in all capitals, and you will start the game with ten lives.

01:26:13.380 --> 01:26:16.380
Really? Yeah, it just, like, it really leans into that shit.

01:26:16.800 --> 01:26:23.920
That's cool. So, I think there's a lot of fun being had here, too, which was kind of what I was getting into about the whole, like, you know, doing spooky but fun.

01:26:24.480 --> 01:26:27.480
No, I think, but does the name you enter do anything?

01:26:27.800 --> 01:26:29.480
No. Is it even listed anywhere?

01:26:29.600 --> 01:26:31.000
It does do something else.

01:26:31.020 --> 01:26:31.860
Sorry, it does.

01:26:32.240 --> 01:26:33.880
I'm trying to think of...

01:26:33.880 --> 01:26:34.940
If you...

01:26:36.100 --> 01:26:38.880
Yeah, your name is at the end of the game, I'm pretty sure.

01:26:40.660 --> 01:26:42.360
They say your name, which is weird.

01:26:42.360 --> 01:26:45.580
And then, um, there are...

01:26:45.580 --> 01:26:50.360
If you do different names, you can also start the game with those characters.

01:26:51.260 --> 01:26:52.220
So, if you do...

01:26:52.220 --> 01:26:53.760
Oh, okay. Oh, cool.

01:26:54.020 --> 01:26:59.520
Okuda, these must be developers of the game, but if you do Okuda, you get to start with Alucard.

01:26:59.860 --> 01:27:01.960
If you do Fujimoto, you get to start with Grant.

01:27:02.280 --> 01:27:04.940
And if you do Urata, you get to start with Sifa.

01:27:04.940 --> 01:27:13.540
Um, and then if you do Akama, uh, A-K-A-M-A, not Akama, uh, then you will...

01:27:13.540 --> 01:27:14.360
I heard the first one.

01:27:14.460 --> 01:27:17.160
Yeah. You will, um, play the second...

01:27:17.160 --> 01:27:18.240
You'll start from the second quest.

01:27:18.400 --> 01:27:22.600
So, that's pretty cool. It's kind of interesting that there's no, like, um, name yourself...

01:27:22.600 --> 01:27:24.120
It's just a hidden password system.

01:27:24.380 --> 01:27:32.860
It is. It is, in a way, because then you can even do, like, um, you know, use this name and, um,

01:27:32.860 --> 01:27:38.600
and use this password, and you can be like, okay, so start with Alucard and be on level

01:27:38.600 --> 01:27:40.060
6-A, you know?

01:27:40.220 --> 01:27:41.920
Like, you can do it that way, too.

01:27:42.120 --> 01:27:43.900
Which is another funny thing about the game.

01:27:44.020 --> 01:27:44.880
Sorry, we're gonna keep going.

01:27:45.460 --> 01:27:52.540
Um, the game has its levels, uh, you know, from 1-1, uh, but there are 10 stages.

01:27:52.800 --> 01:27:58.280
So, when it gets to the 10th stage, instead of it being 10-1, it's A-1.

01:27:58.700 --> 01:28:00.800
Whoa. Couldn't handle the number 10.

01:28:01.160 --> 01:28:02.500
Yeah, they're too many digits.

01:28:02.860 --> 01:28:07.040
Yep, so it went to A, um, for Alucard, of course.

01:28:07.760 --> 01:28:11.160
Um, shoulda went to D for, like, uh, Dracula, right?

01:28:11.660 --> 01:28:12.860
Eh. Yeah.

01:28:13.200 --> 01:28:16.340
I mean, or, but, I mean, Alucard is the one you play it.

01:28:16.340 --> 01:28:17.960
Maybe V for Vampire.

01:28:18.540 --> 01:28:19.600
Hmm. Mm-hmm.

01:28:19.820 --> 01:28:20.680
V for Vendetta.

01:28:21.060 --> 01:28:23.300
Oh. And you know what else V is for?

01:28:23.520 --> 01:28:25.280
The Essential Games List.
